This directory is part of SentinelHost.

Its presence is what tells the scanner to leave this directory alone. Not the directory's
name — anybody can call a directory `sentinel`, and a scanner that trusted the name would
be told what to ignore by whoever it was scanning.

The bridge has to live inside the document root: that is what makes the panel reachable on
hosting with no shell. And it calls exec(), because starting the panel is its whole job —
which is exactly what a PHP malware scanner is built to notice. Without this file, AMWScan
flags the bridge on every cycle, forever, and the tool spends its credibility reporting
itself.

Deleting this file makes the bridge visible to the scan again. That is a reasonable thing
to want: it is code in your document root, and checking that nobody has altered it is not
paranoid. Expect the `Function` finding at `suspicious` if you do.
